  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 319 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nUpper level ridging continues to remain across western CONUS. \nEAsterly flow in the lower levels will promote highs in the low to \nmid 80s across the region Wednesday. A frontal boundary looks to \ndrop south throughout Wednesday and stall out over west-central \nNebraska by the afternoon/evening. This could lead to isolated \nthunderstorm chances across the panhandle near the stalled out \nfront. Thursday heading into Friday, the strengthening ridge will \nmove eastward towards the plains. A upper trough situated over the \npacific ocean will move into southwestern Canada and suppress \nthe upper ridge. This will promote warmer air into the high \nplains. South-southwesterly low level flow and a lifting warm \nfront will increase thunderstorm chances late Thursday night \ninto Friday. The warmer air will also create warmer overall high\ntemperatures across the region with temperatures rising to the \nmid 90s across west central Nebraska Friday afternoon. As the \nupper trough digs down into the Pacific Northwest over the \nweekend, this will flatten out the upper ridge over the Northern\nPlains. The flattening of the ridge will push warmer air further\neast leading to slightly abnormal highs in the low to mid 90s \nacross areas along and west of HWY 83 Saturday and across the \nCWA as a whole Sunday. There has been some signal over a \nshortwave potentially developing across the Central Plains but \ndisagreement among models on whether that timeframe will occur \nduring the early weekend vs the late weekend. Regardless, it is \nsomething to keep an eye on as models become better refined.\n\nThe multiple potential shots of thunderstorm chances through the \nweek and into the weekend should provide a decent amount of \nmeasurable precipitation across the region. Guidance currently \nsuggests a 60 to 80 percent chance of southwest to central Nebraska \nreceiving at least 0.25\" of rainfall through the weekend with that \nprobability dropping to 30 - 50 percent of at least 0.50\". If \nthe thunderstorm chances hold, this should help dampen drought \nconcerns some, especially areas south of I80.",
